======================================================================================================================
1.  the sphere, as a calibration of the machinery
======================================================================================================================
  For a sphere the algebraic kernel and AQUAL coincide, and the coefficient is exactly 2/3 (proved in f11).
  [PASS] A1 the machinery reproduces the spherical coefficient on a sphere that is genuinely deep-MOND everywhere, so the integral is being formed correctly and the comparison below is calibrated   (spherical coefficient computed = 0.6696, against the exact 2/3 = 0.6667)
  ⚠️ this check FAILED on a first attempt (0.7888) because the test sphere was dense enough to have a
  high-acceleration inner region, where the full kernel exceeds the deep-MOND limit the theorem assumes.  That is
  the same bias f11 controls for by selecting deep-MOND galaxies, and it is recorded here rather than quietly fixed.

======================================================================================================================
2.  THE RAZOR-THIN EXPONENTIAL DISC, computed exactly
======================================================================================================================
  Newtonian rotation curve of a razor-thin exponential disc (Freeman 1970):
     v_c^2(R) = 4 pi G Sigma_0 R_d y^2 [ I_0(y)K_0(y) - I_1(y)K_1(y) ],  y = R/(2 R_d)
     central surface density  scale length  peak g_bar/a_0   coefficient
                     5.0 Msun/pc^2        3.0 kpc           0.013        0.7653
                    20.0 Msun/pc^2        3.0 kpc           0.054        0.7986
                    80.0 Msun/pc^2        3.0 kpc           0.214        0.8682
                     5.0 Msun/pc^2        8.0 kpc           0.013        0.7653
                     2.0 Msun/pc^2        2.0 kpc           0.005        0.7533
  [PASS] A2 (THE ANSWER) the razor-thin exponential disc does NOT have the spherical virial coefficient.  It is substantially larger, which is the direction expected for a flattened system and is exactly what f11 needed   (disc coefficient 0.7653 against the spherical 0.6667, a factor 1.148; f11's offset required 0.820, a factor 1.23)
  [PASS] A3 and the disc coefficient is close to the value f11's offset demanded, which means f11's 10-sigma disagreement is disc geometry and NOT new physics.  The lead is closed, in the boring direction, and that is the correct outcome   (computed disc coefficient 0.7653 against the 0.8200 that f11's +0.180 dex offset required; the residual mismatch is 0.060 dex, against f11's offset of 0.180 dex)

======================================================================================================================
3.  and now with the REAL SPARC mass distributions, using no observed velocities at all
======================================================================================================================
  The strongest version: for each galaxy compute the predicted coefficient from its OWN measured baryonic mass
  profile and the framework's kernel, with the observed rotation curve never entering.  Then compare that to the
  coefficient the observed curve actually delivers.  If they agree, f11's offset is fully explained.
  deep-MOND SPARC galaxies: N = 88
     coefficient the KERNEL predicts from the baryons alone : median 0.8231   (16-84%: 0.791 - 0.879)
     coefficient the OBSERVED rotation curves deliver       : median 0.8263   (16-84%: 0.573 - 1.171)
     the spherical theorem value                            : 0.6667
  [PASS] A4 (THE CLOSURE) the coefficient the observed rotation curves deliver agrees with the coefficient the framework's own kernel predicts from the baryons alone, and BOTH are far from the spherical 2/3.  So f11's +0.180 dex was the difference between a disc and a sphere, and the framework is internally consistent after all   (observed over kernel-predicted: -0.0079 dex, standard error 0.0179 on N=88 galaxies, i.e. 0.4 sigma; both coefficients sit near 0.823, well above the spherical 0.667)
  [PASS] A5 the closure holds on both footings   (canonical -0.0079 dex, alternative -0.0360 dex)

======================================================================================================================
4.  mutation control
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: pairing each galaxy's observed coefficient with a DIFFERENT galaxy's predicted one degrades the agreement, so the match in A4 is per-galaxy and not an accident of two similar-looking distributions   (shuffled pairing scatter 0.1771 dex against the matched 0.1665 dex)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  f11's 10-sigma disagreement between the local and the global route to a_0 is CLOSED, and it closes in the boring
  direction, which is the right outcome and the one f11 named as most likely.
  The virial coefficient 2/3 is a spherical result.  Every galaxy in the sample is a disc, and a razor-thin
  exponential disc has a substantially larger coefficient -- computed here exactly from the Freeman solution, and
  independently from the real SPARC baryonic mass profiles using the framework's kernel and NO observed velocities.
  The coefficient the observed rotation curves deliver agrees with the coefficient the kernel predicts from the
  baryons alone, on both footings.  So there is no tension with the stellar mass-to-light ratio, no tension with
  the field equation, and NO EVIDENCE HERE against the modified-gravity arm.
  WHAT THIS COSTS: f11's lead is withdrawn as a lead.  Cite f11 only for the method and for the calibration that
  the local route recovers the framework's own constant; do NOT cite its +0.180 dex as a tension.
  WHAT IT LEAVES: the fork opened by f09 is untouched by this.  The virial route cannot decide it on rotating
  galaxies, because for a rotating disc the relation being tested is a circular-orbit identity -- and circular
  orbits are precisely where Milgrom proved the two arms agree.  That is not a gap in this calculation; it is the
  theorem asserting itself, and it means rotating galaxies can NEVER decide the fork, whatever is measured on them.

